// Attack Surface Reduction events: what each rule stopped or would have stopped, per file and
// per device. One row per rule-outcome-file combination.
//
// AUDITED AND BLOCKED ARE DIFFERENT QUESTIONS AND THIS KEEPS THEM APART. "Audited" is the
// answer to "what breaks if I turn this on"; "Blocked" is the answer to "what broke". The
// ActionType carries both the rule and the outcome in one string - AsrAbusedSystemToolAudited,
// AsrUntrustedExecutableBlocked - so Rule and Outcome are split out rather than left for the
// reader to parse.
//
// THE RULE NAME HERE IS DEFENDER'S, NOT INTUNE'S, and they do not match. Defender calls it
// AsrUntrustedExecutable; Intune calls the same thing "Block executable files from running
// unless they meet a prevalence, age, or trusted list criterion". Join on neither - use
// Get-MsecIntuneAsrRule to see the configured mode, and treat this as the evidence of what
// that configuration actually did.
//
// PASS -Days EXPLICITLY. Search-MsecDefenderHunting defaults to SEVEN days, and the window is
// sent to the API as its own timespan - so an ago(30d) written inside the query is silently
// overridden by it and you get seven days while believing you asked for thirty. Measured on
// one tenant: the same query returned 8 distinct days by default and 31 with -Days 30, and the
// event counts differed by a factor of four. Judging "what would break if I enforce this" on
// an unnoticed seven-day window understates it badly. FirstSeen and Days below are there to
// make the window you actually got visible in the result.
//
// ONE DEVICE CAN DOMINATE, so Devices matters more than Events when judging blast radius -
// measured, a single machine with a non-standard install path produced the large majority of
// one rule's events. Sort on Devices to see spread, on Events to see intensity.
//
// NO RULE OR DEVICE FILTER IN THE QUERY. Rule, DeviceName and FolderPath are columns, so
// narrow in PowerShell - Where-Object Rule -eq 'AsrUntrustedExecutable'. A rule baked in means
// a file per rule and a stale one the day Microsoft adds another.
DeviceEvents
| where ActionType startswith "Asr"
| extend Outcome = case(ActionType endswith "Blocked",  "Blocked",
                        ActionType endswith "Audited",  "Audited",
                        ActionType endswith "Warn",     "Warned",
                        "Other")
| extend Rule = replace_regex(ActionType, @"(Blocked|Audited|Warn)$", "")
| summarize
    Events    = count(),
    Devices   = dcount(DeviceName),
    Days      = dcount(bin(Timestamp, 1d)),
    Accounts  = dcount(InitiatingProcessAccountName),
    FirstSeen = min(Timestamp),
    LastSeen  = max(Timestamp)
    by Rule, Outcome, FileName, FolderPath, InitiatingProcessFileName
| sort by Events desc
